["Three Examples: Using MGPs\n in Educator Evaluations\n Damian Betebenner and Elena Diaz-Bilello\n Center for Assessment\n\nA Presentation for the Teacher Leader Work\n Group","Common Approaches Used to Evaluate\n MGPs in EES\nMost common approaches pursued by\n districts and states using MGPs in educator\n evaluations or for school accountability\n discussed in last meeting:\nOption 1 - Fixed (i.e., MGP based) cuts\nOption 2 - Adding confidence intervals\n around the median SGP or building them\n into the cuts (margin of error)","Examples of both options from 3\n different places\n• Option 1 - Fixed MGPs: New Jersey\n\n• Option 2A – Confidence intervals around\n the median SGP: Denver Public Schools\n\n• Option 2B – Building the margin of error\n into the cuts: Rhode Island","Option 1: New Jersey\n• Set up standard setting panels consisting of\n teachers in different districts\n• Panels Reviewed:\n a. MGP distributions across schools in the\n district\n b. MGP distributions across classrooms in\n the state\n c. Performance profiles of students with\n different SGP levels","New Jersey\n• Panel used performance profiles to identify cuts\n that would recognize different levels of MGP\n growth.\n• First established consensus on what classified\n low, typical or high growth\n• Also set one more cut to differentiate lower\n levels of typical growth from higher levels of\n typical growth\n• Translated MGP values from 1-99 scale to the\n 1-4 rating scale","Other sources consulted\n• In addition to reviewing their own data, NJ\n DOE consulted with other states using a\n similar fixed approach\n• Consulted with the TAC to develop the scale\n\nNote: Have already communicated to\n teachers and administrators that cuts set\n could be adjusted in future years based on\n emerging information and best practices","Points assigned to MGPs in NJ","Option 2A: Denver Public Schools\nLike NJ DOE, groups assembled to review\n growth data and agreed to define cuts\n that establish thresholds for:\na.Low Growth – below 40th percentile\nb.Typical Growth – 40th to below the 60th\n percentile\nc.High Growth – 60th percentile and above","Computed Confidence Intervals (CI) around\na Teacher MGP","Evaluating growth with CIs\n• Look at MGP in each content area\n• The lower limit of CI in each content area\n• The upper limit of CIs in each content area\n• Evaluate these points relative to the cut-\n points used to classify 3 different levels of\n growth\n• Teacher can earn 5 levels of growth\n classified as A (lowest)to E (highest level of\n growth)","The MGP Scoring Model Used in DPS\n\n 40th 60th\n percentile percentile","Considerations: Hard conversations\n about using CIs\n• Technical justifications to use CIs\n• Not an easy task to communicate CI concept\n to the field\n• Decision to go with CIs governed by belief\n that CIs met the “fairness principle” of the\n EES\n\nCurrently, still experiencing difficulty helping\n teachers to understand the CI concept","Option 2B: Rhode Island\n• Rhode Island initially wished to not use a\n confidence interval\n• Considerations of imprecision in the\n median SGPs led to a desire to build in\n some measure of confidence\n• The state decided on using an overall\n confidence interval as opposed to using\n teacher specific confidence intervals","Caterpillar Plots for Hawaii Teachers:\n\n Median SGP < 35 including 1 standard error\n 9.5% of the teachers have median SGP more than 1 standard error\nbelow 35 using BOTH content areas\n\n Median Teacher Student Growth Percentile +/− 1 Standard Error\n\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n 80 ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n\n 9.5%\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●●\n ●\n ●